prose={
586:("To degrade a free person into servitude is to impose a lower status on someone who was not bound to it; Chan speakers use the legal-social wrong as a charge of falsely diminishing what is already present.","Foyan Qingyuan calls excessive explanation 'degrading the free into servitude.' Tianyin Yuanxiu puts the charge into a reply to an elder and also denies that saying everyone possesses the matter is such degradation. Feiyin Tongrong explicitly says equating a bound being with Shakyamuni is neither degrading the free nor calling the base noble, while Yunmen Wenyan makes 'do not degrade the free' a stock counter before a blow. Huayan Shengke turns the same charge against treating Shakyamuni as awake and the assembly as not. These cases bend a status offence into an interview accusation: the named master says the proposed distinction or explanation has needlessly lowered its subject."),
587:("To put on the play when the occasion appears is to have the props ready and perform responsively when a situation calls for it.","Shiyu Mingfang applies the phrase to carrying a fly-whisk into public performance and criticizes the muddy spectacle it produced. Yuan'an Feng, Buhui, Baiyu Si, and Feiyin Tongrong instead use it for responsive teaching action: a no-stringed lute, a no-hole flute, a whisk raised at the right point, or free movement amid the ten directions. The phrase therefore keeps its theatrical scene while Chan records test whether the performance is timely and capable or merely showmanship."),
588:("To draw the bow after the thief has passed is to act only after the decisive moment has already gone.","Xuedou Chongxian applies the line after Zhaozhou's exchange with Huangbo and laughs at the belated move. Yuanwu Keqin gives it as an answer to a follow-up that arrives after the point; Ying'an Tanhua, Yuejiang Zhengyin, Liao'an Qingyu, and Tianyin Yuanxiu likewise use it to mark commentary or rescue that comes too late. The Chan bend is temporal and procedural: a response may be sharp in itself yet miss because the live encounter has already passed."),
589:("To drill a tortoise shell and strike tiles is to seek an answer through divination-like calculation.","Shending Yikui repeatedly dismisses proposed definitions of the 'great man' with 'go ahead and drill the tortoise and strike tiles.' Dahui Zonggao warns his assembly to avoid it; Baiyu Si and Chushi Fanqi answer speculative questions with the phrase; Linji Yixuan uses it after a student's attempted determination. Across the cases it names analytic guessing performed where the public exchange demands a direct response."),
590:("To cover one's ears while stealing a bell is to hide from oneself the noise that exposes one's own act.","Muzhou Daoming says a pious-sounding account resembles covering the ears while stealing a bell. Yuan'an Feng joins it to 'clutching the stolen goods and crying injustice'; Hanyue Fazang calls his interlocutor an ear-covering bell thief and then strikes him. Yuanjie Ying forbids the move, while Konggu Daocheng applies it to people who imagine they have deceived others. Chan usage preserves the self-defeating concealment: the attempted evasion itself advertises what the named critic exposes."),
591:("One blind person leading many blind people is an unseeing guide drawing followers into the same danger.","Yulin Tongxiu, Miyun Yuanwu, Dufeng Benshan, and Daxiu Zhu all complete the image with the blind group being led into a fire pit. Their targets differ—licensed conduct, failure to answer, counterfeit teachers, and untested imitation—but the deployment is stable: someone without the required sight claims authority and multiplies the error publicly. Tian'an Sheng and Shunde use the same line while appraising inherited cases, so the phrase is a verdict on transmission without seeing."),
592:("To draw a snake and add feet is to spoil a sufficient figure by adding what does not belong.","Langting Jingting applies the phrase to describing an incomparable mind with an added moon image. Huanglong Huinan uses it for saying more than one's share requires; Shanfeng Xian calls his own further remark an added foot; Juelang Daosheng says the ancestral style never needed such additions. Yuan'an Feng playfully reverses the result—adding feet makes a dragon—while Danxia Dangui tags an old question as the needless addition. The shared Chan test is whether extra speech clarifies the live matter or merely decorates and distorts it."),
593:("To guard a stump waiting for a rabbit is to repeat the site of a past accident as though the same result must return.","Huanglong Huinan addresses the image to bewildered students; Yuanwu Keqin says later imitators wait at the stump by copying Bodhidharma's silent bow as the formula for receiving the marrow. Po'an Zuxian and Zhongfeng Mingben similarly criticize treating inherited words or postures as fixed proof. Linji Yixuan gives the phrase as his answer for a crowd pressing at the gate. In these cases the stump is a recorded precedent and the awaited rabbit is a result expected from copying it."),
594:("To catch the moon in water is to grasp at a reflected appearance that cannot be seized there.","Tianyin Yuanxiu says asserting that New Year's teaching exists is catching the moon in water. Chushi Fanqi asks how such catching could occur for one who tolerates no grit in the eye; Zhongfeng Mingben uses it in a verse on verbal learning; Ruibai Mingxue pairs it with trying to pluck a flower from a mirror. Zhuanyu Guanheng calls it wasted effort. Chan records retain the reflection scene and apply it to seeking the matter in an image, formulation, or opposed proposition."),
595:("To take a shadow as the thing and lose track of one's head is to follow an image while overlooking what casts it.","Juelang Daosheng says a verse has again taken the shadow and lost the head; Miyun Yuanwu applies the phrase to someone who treats worldly busyness as an external obstacle and thereby abandons himself. Shending Yunwai Ze uses it for a learned man unable to answer about his own household, and Yulin Tongxiu places it among being dazzled by sensory distinctions. Muyun Tongmen contrasts it with carrying inquiry through. The Chan bend is a diagnosis of misplaced reference: attention fixes on derivative appearances while the immediate source goes unrecognized."),
596:("To climb a tree seeking fish is to pursue a goal by a route whose direction cannot reach it.","Zhongfeng Mingben pairs the phrase with guarding a stump when inherited sayings are treated as certificates. Kaifu Daoning applies it to seeking the subtle point through conceptual knowledge; Yuejiang Zhengyin joins it to rigidly waiting for verification. Tianyin Yuanxiu contrasts it with the unobstructed movement of skilled encounter, and Foyan Qingyuan warns students not to wait for it. The records use the impossible search as a procedural criticism: effort continues, but the chosen means and sought result do not meet."),
597:("To throw a brick to draw out jade is to offer something deliberately lesser in hopes of eliciting something finer.","Hanxiu Ruqian and Langting Jingting retell Zhaozhou inviting questions as throwing a brick, only to draw out another clod. Shending Yikui praises an official's attempt to elicit jade but says he still missed his household treasure. Zhufeng Zhenxu applies the image to Shakyamuni eliciting Mahakasyapa, while Tianyin Yuanxiu accepts a layman's letter as a successful brick that reveals his capacity. Chan deployment therefore tests both halves of the tactic: what bait is offered and whether the response is actually jade."),
598:("A poor child remembering old debts is someone with little present capital still occupied by liabilities carried from before.","Dabo Qian makes the phrase a direct answer about the meaning of the western arrival. Chijue Daochong comments with it when an attendant cannot return the rhinoceros fan; Wufeng Ruxue uses it in answer to why Shakyamuni appeared. Tianyin Yuanxiu and Poshan Haiming apply it critically to belated review of old errors or reverence for earlier masters. The Zen bend makes remembered debt a figure for being pulled back into inherited accounts precisely when a present response is required."),
599:("To enter the sea and count its sand is to undertake an immense enumeration that cannot deliver the thing sought.","Huanglong Huinan pairs the image with reciting phrases and recognizing reflected light; Tian'an Sheng quotes the complaint that studying names and forms became sand-counting and counting another's treasure. Guxue Zhe applies it to extensive pilgrimage, while Chaozong Tongren lists classificatory schemes and interpretation as unable to reach the matter. Yuan'an Feng says a mistaken vision leaves later people counting sea-sand. Chan usage targets exhaustive accumulation that remains external to the immediate case."),
600:("The many blind people feeling an elephant each encounter a part and mistake partial contact for the whole animal.","Xiangtian Jinian explicitly reads the elephant comparison as exposing the illness of clinging to one's own view. Dahui Zonggao lists it among cases that must be met personally rather than settled in speech. Xutang Zhiyu says the blind know an elephant yet do not see the whole; an unnamed questioner asks Zhaozhou what the true elephant is when each gives a different account, and Zhaozhou answers that there is no reliance—one simply does not know. The Chan bend turns the comparison into a public test of whether partial descriptions are being promoted as complete seeing.")}
